# os/arch.capabilities — the Arch capability declaration (Core v5, #663/#667).
# ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Read (never sourced) by Core's zsh/02-capabilities.zsh into $_CORE_CAP, and by
# maint/dotfiles-maint.sh, which is bash. bootstrap.sh links it to
# $ZDOTDIR/os.capabilities via blib_link_os_layer.
#
# THE SOURCE of the "Package-manager commands" table in dotfiles-core's
# PORTING-MATRIX.md, Arch column —
# the table is rendered from this file since dotfiles-core#686 (this checkout's vendored
# core/ copy may predate that and still say the reverse): edit here, then run
# `make gen-porting-matrix` in dotfiles-core.
# Validate with:  core/scripts/check-capabilities.sh os/arch.capabilities
#
# THREE KEYS ARE ABSENT ON PURPOSE (PKG_ASSUME_YES, PKG_UPGRADE_PARTIAL,
# MAINT_UNATTENDED_UPGRADE) and each absence is a SAFETY DECLARATION the schema
# honours exactly. Do not add one to be helpful — see the notes at each.
#
# A `#` INSIDE A VALUE IS NOT A COMMENT — the reader keeps it. Notes go above.

# ── package-manager verbs (all required) ──────────────────────────────────────
# LISTED FOR COMPLETENESS, and deliberately NOT aliased anywhere in os/arch.zsh
# (PORTING-MATRIX.md footnote ²³). A bare `-Sy` is safe alone; `-Sy` followed by an
# install is THE partial-upgrade footgun. Core only ever runs this as the refresh
# verb, never as a prefix to an install, which is the one context where it is safe.
PKG_REFRESH=sudo pacman -Sy
PKG_UPGRADE=sudo pacman -Syu
PKG_INSTALL=sudo pacman -S --noconfirm
PKG_REMOVE=sudo pacman -Rns --noconfirm
PKG_SEARCH=pacman -Ss
PKG_OWNS=pacman -Qo
# checkupdates, not `pacman -Qu`: it syncs a copy of the database in USER SPACE and
# never touches the real sync DB, which is what makes counting safe on a rolling
# distro. It comes from pacman-contrib, which install/packages.txt installs
# unconditionally — so naming it here is a promise this repo actually keeps.
#
# --packages WARNS ON THIS LINE AND THE WARNING IS CORRECT TO IGNORE: the validator
# compares the leading BINARY against PACKAGE names, and here they differ
# (checkupdates ships in pacman-contrib). It is a warning and not a failure for
# exactly this case.
PKG_COUNT_PENDING=checkupdates
# checkupdates prints `name oldver -> newver`, one per line: default match and
# field 1 are correct.

# ── what --packages must not nag about (#1087) ────────────────────────────────
# Two reasons, not one. `pacman` is base-system — packages.txt lists what this repo
# ADDS, so the package manager is not in it. `checkupdates` IS installed, under another
# name: it comes from `pacman-contrib`, which packages.txt does list (and annotates
# `# provides checkupdates, paccache, rankmirrors`). The cross-check matches package
# names, not the binaries inside them, so it cannot see that on its own.
PKG_UNLISTED_TOOLS=pacman checkupdates

# ── scheduler (required) ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
SCHEDULER=systemd
SCHEDULER_UNIT_DIR=~/.config/systemd/user

# ── how `up` applies ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# NO PKG_UPGRADE_PRE: `-Syu` refreshes and upgrades in one verb, so a separate
# refresh would be the partial-upgrade window this repo exists to avoid.
#
# NO PKG_ASSUME_YES. pacman's transaction summary is the last thing standing between
# an operator and a 400-package rebuild that replaces a kernel; `up -y` therefore
# behaves like `up` here and lets pacman ask for itself.
#
# NO PKG_UPGRADE_PARTIAL, AND THIS IS THE IMPORTANT ONE. Arch does not support
# upgrading a subset — `pacman -S <pkg>` against a freshly synced DB is precisely
# how a box ends up with mismatched libraries. `up -i` REFUSES on an archive that
# declares no partial verb, which is how this repo says "this must update as a
# whole". Declaring one would turn that refusal into a footgun.

# ── the scheduled runner ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# NO MAINT_UNATTENDED_UPGRADE. A rolling distro that half-upgrades unattended is how
# you get a box that will not boot, and nobody is watching a 03:00 timer when it
# happens. This replaces the `have pacman || have emerge` probe Core used to run —
# which tested for a BINARY standing in for a claim about a DISTRO. Apply with `up`.

# ── optional tools ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# NO TOOLS_OPTIN. PORTING-MATRIX.md's ²¹ cells show Arch PACKAGES jj and ast-grep in
# the official repositories, so there is no delta from Core's _CORE_DOCTOR_OPTIN
# default and a declared list would be a copy that can only drift.
